This entry is weaker than every other one here, and says so rather than
borrowing their confidence. The three cards are recorded as having "arrived from
another system" — commit e4c85ca, which re-allowed their images past a
deny-by-default .gitignore and checked what mattered at the time: one embedded
image each, their own QR, no photograph of anyone, all three deliberately
name-free. It did not record who authored the artwork, and neither does any page,
manifest or later commit. `git log` on those files returns two subjects and no
author of the art.

So this file cannot say the artwork is third-party. What it can say is that
nobody established it was the project's, and LICENSE grants MIT to everything not
named here — which means silence would have kept granting sale rights over
material of unknown origin. The withholding is the conservative direction and it
is cheap to reverse: if the owner confirms the artwork is his, delete this entry
in one edit and the MIT grant resumes untouched.

The card pages carry the same answer in the field a machine reads. Each
src/site/<slug>/index.html #vc-card block now declares `replication`, one of
open / noncommercial / withheld, and these three declare withheld. The default
for an unproven card is withheld, because a record marked unknown is otherwise
exempt from the check that would prove it wrong.

