Unlike every entry above, this material IS this project's to grant. It is
withheld from LICENSE for the opposite reason: the project already granted it on
other terms, in public, and LICENSE contradicts them.

src/site/index.html has carried two tags since the third card landed — "Cards 1,
2, 4 CC BY-NC-4.0" and "Cards 3, 5, 6, 7 MIT" — with a comment beside them
explaining that one tag could not describe two licences. Nothing joined that
claim to the licence files. Measured 2026-08-16, and either result would have
shown this entry was unnecessary: `grep -icE 'manis|aurea|bloom|aurelia|moku|
zaria|kaze' NOTICE` returned 0, and `ls examples/*/LICENSE` returned exactly one
path, examples/moku-card/LICENSE — card 003, the one the page calls MIT. So for
thirty tracked files the landing page said NonCommercial while LICENSE said MIT
over the whole tree unscoped, and MIT is the one that would be read. A stranger
could print and sell all three cards and cite this project's own LICENSE.

The withholding above removes those files from the MIT grant. It puts nothing in
their place, because this file never grants. The NonCommercial terms now sit
beside each card as examples/{manis,aurea,bloom}-card/LICENSE, byte-identical to
Creative Commons' published legal code for CC BY-NC 4.0 (fetched from
creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/legalcode.txt, sha256 41003d4a74749c02…,
19,347 bytes) — the same arrangement MOKU-003 has always had for its MIT, and
placed beside the licence rather than inside it for the reason this file's header
gives.

THIS FAILURE MODE IS NOT THE ONE ABOVE, AND THAT IS WHY IT SURVIVED FOUR PASSES.
Every earlier entry withholds material the project does not own, so each was
found by asking "whose is this?". This one is the project's own work, so that
question clears it every time. The tell was not a missing file — it was two
surfaces describing the same thirty files differently, with nothing joining
them. A public page is a licence claim. When a page and a licence disagree, no
gate notices, because no gate reads prose.


WHAT IS NOT COVERED BY ANY ENTRY ABOVE

Everything not named above is this project's own work and is offered under
LICENSE — named that way round, rather than as a blanket, for the reason the
typeface section gives. The archive entries in src/site/gt/entries.json are the
project's on the only authorship record that exists for them: git, which
attributes every commit that has ever touched that file to this project. If that
is ever wrong for a particular entry, this is the file that has to say so.


WHY THIS FILE HAD TO BE WRITTEN AT ALL

The credit already existed in three places — src/site/gt/index.html (live on the
page), src/site/network.json ("Meta AI, commissioned by the card's owner"), and
the app.js comment above. None of them is a licence carve-out, and that is the
whole lesson: ATTRIBUTION IS NOT A CARVE-OUT. A person who forks this repo reads
LICENSE, receives an unrestricted MIT grant over every byte in the tree, and is
never contradicted by a credit line. Measured before this file existed: the
licence API returned spdx_id MIT for an unscoped LICENSE, and

    git ls-files | grep -iE 'licen[cs]e|notice|copying|copyright|third.?party'

matched exactly two files in 177 tracked — LICENSE and LICENSE-HARDWARE, and
LICENSE-HARDWARE grants rather than withholds. That number deserves distrust in
the direction it was used, so the pattern is printed rather than described: it
does not match OFL.txt, which IS a tracked root-level third-party licence that
scopes material out of MIT. "Nothing in this tree withholds anything" was true of
what the grep saw and false of the tree — the same blanket reflex, one level up,
in the measurement instead of the prose.

All four JPGs were served publicly, 200 with a 404 control, from both the Pages
site and raw.githubusercontent. They still are, and that is the point: the fix
for an over-claim is this file, not withdrawing the bytes the owner wants
printable. The over-claim was live and distributed, never theoretical.

The project already knew the principle and had applied it one class over. The
OFL fonts embedded in src/site/gt/index.html ship their licence WITH the bytes,
because embedding is redistribution — see the comment in tools/build_site.py that
puts OFL.txt on the site. The same reasoning reached the artwork that was embedded
in that same file, and did not reach it at the time, because the fonts arrived with
terms attached and the artwork arrived with a thank-you. (The artwork's bytes left
that file on 2026-08-15; the fonts' did not, and the difference is not licensing —
the fonts are embedded against a THIRD-PARTY request, an argument same-origin
images never had. The withholding is unchanged either way: it follows the artwork,
not the copy.)

An agent that finds this file and wants to "clean it up" into LICENSE: read the
second section again. That edit has already been made once, by someone acting in
good faith, and it broke the machine-readable grant for the whole repository.
